    {
     "id": "notes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "The model behind [Labor Market Equilibrium and Wage Floor](note:labor-market-equilibrium-and-wage-floor) and [Labor Tax Wedge](note:labor-tax-wedge), which name this note and add the panels. Place those views, not this note."
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- **Model.** The example of [Classical Labor Market Model](note:classical-labor-market-model) with $p = 1$: 600 consumers each supply $h = (w - \\tau_w)/6$ hours ([Labor-Leisure Choice](note:labor-leisure-choice)), so $L^S = 100(w - \\tau_w)$; one firm with $F(L) = a\\ln L$ demands $L^D = a/(w + \\tau_f)$ ([Labor Demand](note:labor-demand)).\n- **Equilibrium.** With $\\tau = \\tau_w + \\tau_f$ and take-home wage $w_n = w - \\tau_w$, clearing $100w_n = a/(w_n + \\tau)$ gives $w_n = \\left(\\sqrt{\\tau^2 + 4a/100} - \\tau\\right)/2$. Only $\\tau$ enters, which is the point of [Tax Incidence](note:tax-incidence).\n- **Wage floor.** The posted wage is $\\max(\\bar{w}, w^*)$; at a binding floor hours worked are $L^D$ and unemployment is $L^S - L^D$.\n- **Tests.** The lecture's example ($w^* = 20$, $L^* = 2{,}000$; floor at 25) and computed cases (taxes of 5, $a = 60{,}000$), each solved independently in Python by root-finding."
      }
     ],
